                <h2>This Week's Shift: Traffic Costs Are Diverging, Budget Migration Window Opens</h2>
<p>Based on data from the 以渔数媒投放实验室 analyzing 200+ DTC client accounts, a notable signal emerged this week in the overseas ad market:</p>
<p><strong>Meta (Facebook) traffic costs continue climbing</strong>, with CPC up ~12% from last month's average. For competitive niches, CPA has breached LTV warning thresholds. Meanwhile, <strong>TikTok and Google inventory became relatively affordable</strong>, with CPMs dropping 8%-15% YoY. TikTok US feed ads in particular showed CTR 30%+ above market average.</p>
<p>What does this mean? The "All in Meta" single-channel strategy is losing effectiveness. The budget migration window is open.</p>
<h2>Impact Analysis: Three Types of Teams Face Different Pressures</h2>
<h3>1. Meta-Dependent Teams</h3>
<p>Those placing 80%+ budget on Meta face the most pressure this week. Rising CPC combined with iOS privacy headwinds are pushing ROAS curves rightward. One tool App advertiser noted: "Same budget, 2.8 ROAS last week, now down to 2.1."</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Start TikTok and Google testing immediately at 10% budget allocation. Don't wait until the account is struggling.</p>
<h3>2. Multi-Channel Teams</h3>
<p>These teams are actually benefiting this week. A home goods DTC seller reported: "TikTok CPA is only 60% of Meta. Shifting budget there brought overall CAC down 18%."</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Scale verified TikTok and Google ad sets while monitoring TikTok Shopping beta access.</p>
<h3>3. New Independent Site Projects</h3>
<p>Limited capital means low error tolerance. This week's shift is actually positive: lower testing costs mean more data per dollar spent.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Allocate 30%-40% to testing, prioritize TikTok feed + Google Shopping combination.</p>
<h2>Tactical Framework: The 'Three-Blade' Dynamic Budget Model</h2>
<p>Based on this week's observations, 以渔数媒 recommends a <strong>dynamic three-phase budget allocation model</strong>:</p>
<h3>Blade 1: Testing Phase (20%-30%)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Distributed testing across 2-3 ad sets per platform</li>
<li>Diverse creative formats: carousel, video, Collection Ads</li>
<li>$50-100 daily budget per ad set for 7-day data accumulation</li>
</ul>
<h3>Blade 2: Scaling Phase (50%-60%)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Double down on test winners exceeding ROAS thresholds</li>
<li>Scale in 20% increments to avoid platform re-learning</li>
<li>Enable auto-rules to pause when ROAS drops below 1.5</li>
</ul>
<h3>Blade 3: Harvest Phase (15%-25%)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prioritize retargeting cart abandoners</li>
<li>Pair with abandoned checkout emails to shorten decision cycles</li>
<li>Accept higher CPA for high-LTV customers</li>
</ul>
<h2>以渔 Tip: Account Balance Management Matters</h2>
<p>Even perfect budget allocation fails if the account runs dry. Enable <strong>balance alert mechanisms</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li>Alert when main account balance falls below 5x daily budget</li>
<li>Set up auto-reload rules to ensure uninterrupted delivery</li>
<li>Prioritize reload allocations by channel performance</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><strong>Wrap-up</strong>: This week's shift isn't random—platform competition naturally drives cost divergence. Rather than complaining about rising costs, optimize your budget model. Dynamic allocation, channel diversification, and meticulous account management will steady your ROAS despite market volatility.</p>
